On 2006-06-22, Gavin77 wrote:
> Pet does not always follow you into places

As others have said, your options to influence this are: Treats in main
inventory, magic whistle, leash.

> It is often hard to get your Pet next to the staircase in a dungeon
> Pets often do nothing when they are next to you and you have multiple
> monsters attacking you. They only seem to help sometimes ??

I can only think of two reasons for a pet not to help you; first, when it's
busy eating. This is somewhat controllable -- make sure your pet doesn't
get to a corpse or food item when there's baddies approaching you.

The second reason is that the opponents are of a higher level than your
pet, or your pet is badly hurt. If you have a stethoscope, you can
investigate this. Try to guess wether your pet is injured, and heal it
appropriately (spell of healing, wielded potion). 

Also make sure that your pet levels up sufficiently. Pets also do this by 
killing monsters, but for them, every kill is worth the same. So let them 
have all the grid bugs and jackals in the first levels, since they're only 
worth 1 XP to you. Generally let your pet participate in the fights while 
the monsters are still easy, to make sure it can help you further down when
they get tougher. (This advice is especially important for weaker classes
like wizards, barbarians should generally be able to fight on their own).

